problems.problem:
A city has $57$ bus routes. It is known that:

1) from any stop one can get to any other stop without changing;

2) for any pair of routes there is one, and only one, stop at which one can change from one of these routes to the other;

3) each route has at least three stops.

How many stops does each of the $57$ routes have?

A bus route has $14$ stops (counting the two terminal ones). At most $25$ passengers can ride in the bus at the same time. Prove that during a trip of the bus from one end to the other:

a) there will be eight different stops $A_1$, $B_1$, $A_2$, $B_2$, $A_3$, $B_3$, $A_4$, $B_4$ such that no passenger rides from $A_1$ to $B_1$, no passenger rides from $A_2$ to $B_2$, no passenger rides from $A_3$ to $B_3$ and no passenger rides from $A_4$ to $B_4$;

b) it may happen that the passengers ride in such a way that there are no $10$ different stops $A_1$, $B_1$, $A_2$, $B_2$, $A_3$, $B_3$, $A_4$, $B_4$, $A_5$, $B_5$ having similar properties.

Which of the two numbers is larger:

a) $2^{2^{\cdot^{\cdot^{\cdot^{2}}}}}$ ($n$ twos) or $3^{3^{\cdot^{\cdot^{\cdot^{3}}}}}$ ($n-1$ threes);

b) $3^{3^{\cdot^{\cdot^{\cdot^{3}}}}}$ ($n$ threes) or $4^{4^{\cdot^{\cdot^{\cdot^{4}}}}}$ ($n-1$ fours)?

Inside a tetrahedron there is a triangle whose projections onto the $4$ faces of the tetrahedron have areas $P_1$, $P_2$, $P_3$, $P_4$. Prove that:

a) in a regular tetrahedron $P_1\leqslant P_2+P_3+P_4$;

b) if $S_1$, $S_2$, $S_3$, $S_4$ are the areas of the corresponding faces of the tetrahedron, then $P_1S_1\leqslant P_2S_2+P_3S_3+P_4S_4$.

A device for comparing the numbers $\log_a b$ and $\log_c d$ ($a, b, c, d>1$) works according to the rules:

– if $b>a$ and $d>c$, it passes to comparing the numbers $\log_a\frac{b}{a}$ and $\log_c\frac{d}{c}$;

– if $b<a$ and $d<c$, it passes to comparing the numbers $\log_d c$ and $\log_b a$;

– if $(b-a)(d-c)\leqslant 0$, it gives the answer.

a) Show how the device will compare the numbers $\log_{25}75$ and $\log_{65}260$.

b) Prove that it will compare any two unequal logarithms in a finite number of steps.

There are stones in boxes. In one move a number $k$ is chosen; then the stones in each box are divided into groups of $k$ stones and a remainder of fewer than $k$ stones. One stone from each group and the whole remainder are left in the box. Is it possible to achieve in $5$ moves that exactly one stone remains in each box, if each of them contains

a) at most $460$ stones;

b) at most $461$ stones?

Can the edges of an $n$-gonal prism be coloured in $3$ colours so that every face contains all $3$ colours and at every vertex edges of different colours meet, if

a) $n=1995$;

b) $n=1996$?

About a continuous function $f$ it is known that:

(a) $f$ is defined on the whole number line;

(b) $f$ has a derivative at every point (and thus the graph of $f$ has a unique tangent at every point);

(c) the graph of the function $f$ contains no points one of whose coordinates is rational and the other irrational.

Does it follow that the graph of $f$ is a straight line?

Is it possible to assemble in space a closed chain of $61$ identical, consistently rotating gears so that the angles between meshed gears are at least $150^\circ$? Here:

(a) for simplicity the gears are regarded as circles;

(b) gears are meshed if the corresponding circles have a common tangent at the point of contact;

(c) the angle between meshed gears is the angle between the radii of their circles drawn to the point of contact;

(d) the first gear must be meshed with the second, the second with the third, and so on, the 61st with the first, and the other pairs of gears must have no common points.

In a round-robin chess tournament every participant played every other once. Call a game irregular if the player who won it ended up with fewer points than the player who lost it. (A win gives $1$ point, a draw $1/2$, a loss $0$.) Can irregular games make up

a) more than $75\%$ of the total number of games in the tournament;

b) more than $70\%$?

The following operation is performed with a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$: one of the given vertices is replaced by the point symmetric to this vertex with respect to the perpendicular bisector of a diagonal (of which it is not an endpoint), the new point being denoted by the former letter. This operation is applied successively to the vertices $A, B, C, D, A, B, \ldots$, $n$ times in all. Call a quadrilateral admissible if its sides are pairwise distinct and it remains convex after any number of operations. Does there exist:

a) an admissible quadrilateral that becomes equal to the original one after $n<5$ operations;

b) a number $n_0$ such that every admissible quadrilateral becomes equal to the original one after $n=n_0$ operations?

The following operation is performed with a convex quadrilateral $ABCD$: one of the given vertices is replaced by the point symmetric to this vertex with respect to the perpendicular bisector of a diagonal (of which it is not an endpoint), the new point being denoted by the former letter. This operation is applied successively to the vertices $A, B, C, D, A, B, \ldots$, $n$ times in all. Call a quadrilateral admissible if its sides are pairwise distinct and it remains convex after any number of operations. Does there exist:

a) an admissible quadrilateral that becomes equal to the original one after $n<5$ operations;

b) a number $n_0$ such that every admissible quadrilateral becomes equal to the original one after $n=n_0$ operations?

Misha has mentally placed inside a given circle of unit radius a convex polygon containing the centre of the circle, and Kolya tries to guess its perimeter. In one step Kolya points out some line to Misha and learns from him whether it intersects the polygon. Does Kolya have the possibility of surely guessing the perimeter of the polygon:

a) in $3$ steps with accuracy $0.3$;

b) in $2007$ steps with accuracy $0.003$?

A team of $n$ schoolchildren takes part in a game: each of them is given a hat of one of $k$ colours known in advance, and then at a whistle all the schoolchildren simultaneously choose a scarf for themselves. The team gets as many points as there are participants whose hat colour coincides with the colour of their scarf (there are enough scarves and hats of every colour; during the game each participant does not see his own hat but sees the hats of all the others, and has no right to give out any information until the whistle). What is the largest number of points that the team, having planned in advance the actions of each of its members, can be guaranteed to get:

a) for $n=k=2$;

b) for arbitrary fixed $n$ and $k$?

Sasha has written around a circle, in arbitrary order, at most one hundred distinct natural numbers, and Dima is trying to guess how many there are. To do this, Dima tells Sasha several positions in some order, and then Sasha tells Dima, in the same order, which numbers stand at the positions indicated by Dima, if the numbers are counted clockwise starting from one and the same number. Can Dima be sure to guess the number of numbers written by Sasha by naming

a) $17$ positions;

b) fewer than $16$ positions?
